#Setting up Multiversal Hub
	*mkdir git-branch-lab && cd git-branch-lab
	*git init
	*echo "# Git Branch Lab" > README.md
	*echo "This is my hub for multiversal Git branch experiments" >> README.md
	*git add README.md
	*git commit -m "Initial commit: Create the multverse hub"

#Creating Your First Alternate Reality
	*git branch feature-dimension --> this command creates a new branch but DOESN'T switch to it yet
	*git branch --> the '*' shows which branch you are currently attached to
	*git checkout feature-dimension -->this command switches your current dir. to the feature-dimension branch and it updates your working directory to match the state of the feature-dimension branch...its like steppping through a portal into the new reality
	*git switch feature-dimension --> newer prefered command specifically designed for branch switching 
---Creating and switching branches in one command 
	*git checkout -b feature-dimension 
	*git switch -c feature-dimension

#Shaping Your New Reality
	*echo "This is a powerful artifact from another dimension" > dimesional-artifact.txt
	*git add dimensional-artifact.txt && git commit -m "Created a powerful interdimensional artifact"
	*echo "#### Feature Dimension" >> README.md
	*echo "We've discovered a powerful artifact in this reality" >> README.md
	*git add README.md && git commit -m "Document the discovery of the artifact"
	*git diff master

#Merging Realities
	*git checkout master --> jump back to our main reality
	*git switch master
	*git merge feature-dimension

#Closing Dimensional Portals
	*git branch -d feature-dimension
		'-d': tells git to delete the branch but only if its been fully merged
			- Git will only allow you to delete a branch using the -d if the changes in it have been merged to the branch you are on
			- If you try to delete a branch that hasn't been fully mereged git will give you a warning

